course: dictionary
lesson: 12266
title: Words that touch
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Contiguous is a formal word. It means two things touch each other or share a border, with no gap between them. Look at the clue words around it. «The two gardens are contiguous, sharing one long fence.» The fence is shared, so the gardens must be touching.
explain: Contiguous can also mean next to each other in a row or in time, one straight after another. «The class had five contiguous lessons, with no break between them.» No break means the lessons came one straight after another.
explain: You will not often hear people say contiguous when they talk. They say touching, next to or joined. When you meet contiguous in a textbook or an exam, swap in touching or next to and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Contiguous means touching, or sharing a border, with no gap.
fact: Contiguous can also mean next to each other in a row, or one straight after another in time.
fact: In geography, «the contiguous United States» means the 48 states that touch each other. Alaska and Hawaii are left out.
fact: In maths and computing, contiguous blocks or numbers sit side by side without gaps.
fact: Contiguous is not compared. You cannot say more contiguous or most contiguous; something is either contiguous or it is not.
exercise: «The two gardens are contiguous, with no gap or fence between them.» What does contiguous mean here? | *Touching, with no gap | Far apart across the town | Hidden behind a tree | Broken into small pieces
exercise: «The contiguous United States has 48 states.» Which states are not part of the contiguous United States? | Ohio and Maine | California and Oregon | Texas and Florida | *Alaska and Hawaii
exercise: «The class had five contiguous lessons, one straight after another.» What does contiguous mean in this sentence? | Very loud and noisy | Cancelled for the day | Spread out across the whole year | *One straight after another
test: In everyday talk, which word could you use instead of contiguous? | *Joined | Hidden | Far | Lost
test: In maths, «contiguous numbers» means numbers that sit how? | Scattered with big gaps between them | *Side by side with no gaps | Bigger than ten | Written in words only
exam: Which sentence uses contiguous correctly? | I felt more contiguous after my swim | The dog was very contiguous and barked | The bell rang contiguous at noon | *The two fields are contiguous, touching along one long edge
